==Question #1==
{{Cquote2|Why are you implementing Fresh Start Servers, who are they for, and how does it benefit the game and the playerbase?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HPRsYQX5Avk&t=350s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Most of this has been covered in the FAQ, but to reiterate the "why". We did some research, and you may have seen a survey that we put out, which indicated that this type of fresh start service could appeal to those players. Given that they require minimal dev time, we just thought "why don't we test out the theory and see whether we're right or not?"
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Regarding the "who", I think this is a crucial one to cover. It's a large topic of conversation within the community. Fresh Start Worlds are aimed at new players or players who haven't been on Old School for a long time. It's not intended to be content that attracts you away from the main game. I'm sure people will be drawn to it - some people will want to fight for the highscores and just experience that fresh start.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Fresh Start Worlds is aimed at people who don't currently play; who don't have existing accounts. One thing that has been overlooked is the power of having so many people at the same stage of the game together.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' If you were to throw a returning plyayer back into the current game, they're going to be met with heaps of players who are high-level and have so much gear. They'll go to an earlier game area and find that it will have much less activity.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Fresh Start worlds offer an opportunity for like-minded players at a similar level of experience and progression - as they'll all be starting again - to play together and experience that without the interruption of much higher-level players.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I'm sure you've seen it before; some people will stand out at Lumbridge, and these people will hand out hundreds of k's worth of gear to help a player start their journey on day one. While it's lovely to see this, the other side of being given this help is that it opens up more of the game than intended to new players; it just sometimes adds extra confusion.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Regarding the benefits, it's always good for new players to come into the game! It helps us identify any pain points better than we can at the moment in the early stages of progression. This is something that would hopefully cause a lot of new players to come into the game at one moment as opposed to them trickling in when their feedback could be lost.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So from a CM perspective, I'm hopeful that it will help us identify pain points so we can look to improve for future new players.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I've been reading many of the comments posted on Reddit and social media across the internet. I'll give a little insight into my thinking on what we're trying to do with Old School's Fresh Start Worlds.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' One of the issues is if you're a new player, when is the point you want to join? All of you have joined at some point - you can join any day of the year - but when you think of other MMOs, like World of Warcraft, there's a very clear point to join; when the expansions come out. You're not going to join World of Warcraft today; you're going to join it around the time the expansion comes out. You're going to join alongside other people. World of Warcraft gets to reimagine how this sort of progression through the game is thought of.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' With Old School, it's very difficult to create that really strong point in time to say, "Okay, if you were interested in Old School, join now. You'll be surrounded by other people at the same time, part of a community and so on." So the thinking is to create a mode like Fresh Start where you can temporarily be in a separate server playing alongside other people, and it's a good chance to do so.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I say this as we're looking primarily at brand new people and people who maybe haven't played Old School for a long time or played RuneScape back in the day and want to come back today. That's where I feel like it could be effective.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Now, that's yet to be proven. This is a bit of an experiment, and we'll learn along the way. Primarily, I don't want to move people from main Old School to this, nor do I really want people to feel like they want to create a second account to play this. That's not what we intend to do with Fresh Start Worlds.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' That's where I feel it is very different to Leagues and other game modes. This is about trying to create something that can be of interest to new players or long-lapsed plauers. That's why I think it is the way that it is. You'll come along, and this is why you're starting a new account - because it's aimed at new people completely.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' That's also why we want to eventually merge it into the main game after six months. This is your entry point - it's a way to get into the game, stick around and join the wider community at a point of your choosing.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Certainly, it's been a lot of reading and a lot of thinking about the different points people have been making online. It's all stuff we're processing and really trying to think about and understand.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' One of the leading concerns is this point about the economy after six months. We still need to think more about these things and make sure that we are okay with that happening and if we think the economy will be good after that change.

==Question #2==
{{Cquote2|Additional game modes can segment the Old School community whilst also taking up valuable dev resources away from the main game. Are Fresh Start Servers going to take up a lot of dev resources from the main game? Is leadership concerned about the number of game modes that are in OSRS?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HPRsYQX5Avk&t=740s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Markos:''' Fresh Start servers is a relatively cheap update, as Kieren said a moment ago. We don't expect to take up that much resource. It will be done relatively quickly compared to most of the updates we do. As you would have seen, the design is relatively simple with what we've implemented.
* '''Mod Markos:''' We generally see game modes as having a clear value in the sense that they give players an opportunity to re-experience parts of the game in different ways from what they've seen before. Things like Leagues are also quite good at creating a strong moment in time, which gets people back into the game and encourages them to try out again.
* '''Mod Markos:''' We do recognise there's a balance to be had. We must ensure that while we do some game modes, there will always be content going to the main game that’s fun and engaging.
* '''Mod Markos:''' For 2022 overall, we had a League in January, and now we're about to have the Fresh Start Worlds in October, so we've had two kinds of modes to bookend the year. We've also had some conversations about speed-running, which is a game mode but a very different experience.
* '''Mod Markos:''' In terms of what we want to do in the future, we'll probably look at a similar-ish balance. We've got a provisional roadmap for 2023 that we'll give more detail in a future livestream, but we would probably be looking to strike a similar balance, where the vast majority of the updates will be going into the main game, and there are maybe a couple of game modes across the year.
* '''Mod Markos:''' One of the things we've spoken about before is what we do with things like Deadman in the future when we are trying to think about what we could do to refresh that a bit.

